7	// function or it is a bug: with no `default:` arm, an unenumerated name falls off
8	// the end and the mapping returns `undefined`.
9	//
10	// `undefined` is the worst possible answer here because nothing downstream
11	// notices. The dialog policy branches on the kind — "was the mic refused?", "did
12	// we abort on purpose?" — and every one of those comparisons is simply false, so
13	// the policy takes its do-nothing arm. Nothing throws, nothing logs, the UI keeps
14	// showing a live session, and the user is heard by nobody. That is this pack's
15	// whole subject: speech failures are silent, so every path must produce exactly
16	// one observable outcome, including the path a browser invented after you shipped.
17	//
18	// PARSED, NOT GREPPED — three ways, each killing a false alarm a text scan makes:
19	//
20	//   1. Case labels are read at the switch body's OWN depth. A `default:` belonging
21	//      to a nested switch inside one arm does not count as this switch's default,
22	//      and a nested switch's cases don't count toward this one's labels.
23	//   2. Only a MAPPING switch is judged — one whose arms `return` a value. A switch
24	//      that dispatches side effects over error names is a different shape with a
25	//      different (often legitimate) reason to omit a default, and this rule does
26	//      not have an honest opinion about it.
27	//   3. A `return`/`throw` immediately after the switch IS the fallback. The
28	//      idiomatic total mapping puts the catch-all there rather than in a `default:`
29	//      arm, and it is exactly as total. Firing on it would be pure noise — and is
30	//      the single false alarm a naive "no `default` in this file" grep makes most.
